y to  survive  we might be days away from that people  that are returning from the christmas  holiday  are going to start feeling ill in the  next few days  holiday travel risks creating a viral  wildfire  1.2 million travelers passed through u.s  airports on sunday alone  the highest number since the start of  the pandemic at a time when the virus is  everywhere  as we get into the next few weeks it  might actually get worse  december was the deadliest month of the  pandemic in the u.s  january could be even worse and there  are fears that a new more contagious  variant of the virus detected in the uk  is circulating unchecked on american  soil  i do think it's probably here we don't  know we're not monitoring for it in the  way that the uk  and other countries are all right here  we go all the while the great hope  a vaccine is rolling out much slower  than expected  the trump administration's operation  warp speed had promised to deliver 20  million doses by the end of 2020  three days before the new year and only  two million people have been vaccinated  there's no doubt about it that things  are going a little bit more slowly than  we want  too little too late for the thousands of  dead and for the severely ill  in san antonio texas covit 19 patient  james telschick  is now waiting for a double lung  transplant the disease will  keep trying to pull you in  and you just gotta you gotta keep  fighting  in this fight doctors warn the toughest  days are still to come  jackson prosco global news washington  ahead nearly 40 years later a secret  notebook is secret  no more but where is the young writer  now  the canadian press has named canadian  soccer captain christine sinclair as its  female athlete of the year  netting her second bobby rosenfeld award  the honor caps an all-time  record-breaking year for the two-time  olympic bronze medalist after she marked  186  overall international goals that is the  most of  any soccer player in the world a holiday  miracle this past weekend surrounding  the top secret diary of a 10 year old  girl  nick guns who happens to be a historian  found this notebook in the crawl space  of his parents toronto home  it came with a dire warning do not read  guns didn't he just flip through it for  a name and an age  turns out it belongs to a girl named  alison jenkins  in 1983 he posted a photo on social  media  and he found her jenkins is now a  singer-songwriter and a music teacher  living in vancouver  the week after christmas can be a little  bit of a anti-climax  and so to have something like this to  kind of keep me busy and keep me amused  it's been lovely  i mean just hundreds and hundreds of  people came online to  to take this on and help and and find a  person and that's just  so um so reassuring  the notebook will be mailed out to  vancouver tomorrow  next recognizing the everyday heroes  still  risking their safety to keep all of us  going  [Music]  2020 is the year thousands of people  perfected the wfh  experience working from home but when  you really think about it  that's a privilege essential workers  have to show up in person  to keep this country functioning keep us  fed  and keep us alive donna friesen reflects  on their role  in this pandemic so much of our humanity  rests  in our hands who we hold how we comfort  and how we use them for work among the  heroes of 2020 are those in the eye of  the storm  the doctors and nurses for them every  day is about life and death  it is as demanding and essential as it  gets  in those moments where there's high  stress you know  every family member just wants to be  there holding their hand and they can't  in the spring we began to salute them  using our hands  to applaud them for these heroes  getting up every day getting back in the  fight getting back to the front lines  comes at a great price it comes  with a personal sacrifice nevertheless  every day these brave men and women  show up and it dawned on us health care  workers  aren't the only ones using their hands  to help us  if you're working in the grocery store  during the pandemic i want you to know  you're making a real difference i'm sure  it hasn't always been easy for you  but your work is essential thousands of  others are doing essential work too  when we hoarded food they kept the  shelves stocked  kept the checkout lines moving thousands  of people doing work that isn't  glamorous or well paid  risking their own health to serve us and  help us  our social workers transition house  staff  our home care workers and all those  trying to assist  people in need when you're out on the  street rescuing  women or children or feeding the hungry  or providing support to the disabled  community  a lot of their work is still face to  face  and they are still showing up every day  showing up to care feed and comfort our  elders and care homes  to deliver our packages drive the buses  collect our garbage teach our children  all of it hands-on work that keeps our  world in motion  and integrates so seamlessly into our  modern lives  we tend to take it for granted i hope  that one of the things  that this pandemic has taught us  is that people who do some of the work  which is  most essential for our actual  our literal survival are among  the lowest paid people in our country  it is a question of value to quote oscar  wilde  do we know the price of everything and  the value of nothing  when this coveted crisis is over there  are deeper questions to tackle  about the worth of heartfelt hands-on  work  for now what we offer those doing  essential exhausting work is profound  gratitude  donna friesen global news  and that is global national for this  monday night i'm farah nasser  tonight's york canada is peachland  british columbia thank you so much for  watching take care of yourselves  and each other have a good night
 

